<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lower Rhine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> is the section of the Rhine below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, where the river leaves the Rhenish Massif and flows across the North German Plain through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>; the name also denotes the surrounding region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> and Duisburg lie along it. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a land of powerful territorial princes and church lords, above all the archbishops of Cologne and the counts and dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> and Jülich.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lower-rhine.jpg" alt="Lower Rhine" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BAW_Bundesanstalt für Wasserbau · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%BCsseldorf_Rhein_(51720326377).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
